# If you prefer the allow list template instead of the deny list, see community template:
# https://github.com/github/gitignore/blob/main/community/Golang/Go.AllowList.gitignore
#
# ── A note on anchoring, which this file has been bitten by ────────────────────
#
# git's rule: a pattern containing a slash ANYWHERE except at the end is
# relative to this file's directory. A pattern with no slash — or with only a
# trailing slash — matches at EVERY depth. So `data/` is not "the data
# directory", it is "any directory named data, anywhere".
#
# That difference is invisible until it costs you a day. A bare `server` here
# once matched `cmd/server/`, so every new file added under it was skipped
# silently by `git add -A` — and `git status` says nothing about a path it has
# been told to ignore. It is `/server` now, and the audit that fixed it went
# through every other slash-less pattern in this file.
#
# The rule this file now follows:
#
#   - a pattern that means ONE path gets a leading slash;
#   - a pattern that is genuinely global (a build-artifact extension, a tool's
#     cache directory) stays bare AND carries a comment saying why.
#
# `TestGitignore_NoUnreviewedBarePatterns` in internal/repohygiene enforces it:
# a new slash-less pattern fails the suite by name unless it is added to that
# test's allowlist with a reason. Add the reason, not an exemption.
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Binaries for programs and plugins.
# Bare and global on purpose: a compiled object is never committed, at any depth.
*.exe
*.exe~
*.dll
*.so
*.dylib

# Test binary, built with `go test -c`.
# Bare and global: `go test -c` writes it into whatever package directory you
# ran it from, so anchoring would defeat it.
*.test

# Code coverage profiles and other test artifacts.
#
# `*.out` and `*.coverprofile` are bare and global: `go test -coverprofile` and
# the pprof `-cpuprofile`/`-memprofile` flags write next to wherever they were
# invoked. Hazard worth knowing, since it is the same class this file's header
# describes: a golden fixture named `testdata/expected.out` WOULD be silently
# ignored. There is none today; if you add one, give it a different extension
# rather than negating here.
#
# `coverage.*` and `profile.cov` are anchored: they name specific root
# artifacts (`make test-coverage` writes coverage.out and coverage.html at the
# root), and unanchored `coverage.*` matched `docs/coverage.md` — a plain
# documentation file this repo could reasonably have added at any time.
*.out
*.coverprofile
/coverage.*
/profile.cov

# Dependency directories (remove the comment below to include it)
# vendor/

# Go workspace file.
# Anchored: a go.work is only meaningful at or above the module root, and a
# file of that name deeper in the tree is not one.
/go.work
/go.work.sum

# env file.
# Bare and global on purpose: a `.env` is a secret wherever it appears —
# web/.env would be exactly as dangerous as the root one. The negations are
# bare for the same reason, so they keep tracking the committed samples at
# whatever depth a future one lands.
.env
!.env.test
!.env.example

# Editor/IDE
# .idea/
# .vscode/

# Build output directory.
# Anchored: `make build` writes bin/azimuthal and `make e2e-test` writes
# bin/azimuthal-e2e.exe, both from the repository root. Unanchored it also
# matched any nested bin/ — a plausible directory name for committed helper
# scripts.
/bin/

# Locally built CLI binaries (build outputs of cmd/*; never commit compiled artifacts)
/migrate
/azimuthal

# Frontend
web/node_modules/
web/playwright-report/
web/test-results/

# Local data dirs (RSA key persistence path).
# Anchored: the root `data/` is the key persistence path, and the second line
# below already names the other one explicitly — which is itself the evidence
# that root was always the intent. Unanchored, `data/` matched `web/src/data/`,
# a directory a frontend change could introduce any week.
/data/
cmd/server/data/

# Claude Code local settings (never commit).
# Bare and global: worktrees and nested checkouts each get their own.
.claude/

# CI progress tracking (private repo only — never push to public)
#
# CLAUDE.md was removed from this list in the post-P3 reconciliation, by
# maintainer decision. It had been ignored here since the private/public split,
# which is why the file never existed despite every phase prompt since P0
# opening with "read CLAUDE.md" — git silently skipped it on every `git add`.
# The committed CLAUDE.md is assembled only from material that is already
# public (spec §2 and §10, the Makefile, ci.yml) and carries no secret and no
# unfixed-vulnerability detail. Keep it that way: it is a public file.
#
# The three progress directories are anchored: each names one directory at the
# root, and none of them is a name that should disappear from a nested path.
github-actions-progress/.gitleaks.toml
docs/agent-briefs.md
docs/github-setup-checklist.md
docs/project-state.md
docs/regression-test-checklist.md
scripts/local-test.sh
scripts/push-private.sh
scripts/push-public.sh
scripts/release-private.sh
scripts/release-public.sh
scripts/remote-test-host-deploy.sh
.github/workflows/release-private.yml
/current-agent-progress/
/github-actions-progress/
/release-progress/
/server

# Local golangci-lint cache (set via GOLANGCI_LINT_CACHE when parallel
# sessions share a checkout — never committed).
#
# The glob rather than the exact name: a per-worktree cache wants a
# per-worktree directory, and `.golangci-cache-<topic>/` did not match the bare
# form. 4431 cache files reached a commit here before this was widened, because
# `git add -A` has no opinion about what a directory is for.
#
# Bare and global: a worktree anywhere under the checkout gets its own.
.golangci-cache*/
